# Layering rules

Read `docs/02-architecture.md` and `docs/03-project-structure.md` before
adding files.

## Layer responsibilities

| Layer | Path | Owns |
|---|---|---|
| Domain | `app/domain/` | Entities, enums, repository interfaces (ABCs). Pure Python; no IO. |
| Application | `app/application/` | Use cases (orchestration), application services. |
| Infrastructure | `app/infrastructure/` | Implementations: DB, Redis/arq, storage, providers, Telegram sender. |
| Bot | `app/bot/` | Telegram handlers, keyboards, callbacks, middleware. Thin shells over use cases. |
| API | `app/api/` | FastAPI routes (`/healthz`, `/readyz`, `/d/{token}`). |
| Workers | `app/workers/` | Long-running OS processes (cleanup, backup); `app/main_worker.py` for arq. |
| Composition | `app/composition.py` | DI root — wires concrete implementations to interfaces. |
| Utils | `app/utils/` | Pure helpers (URL parsing, filenames, correlation, retries). No IO. |

## Allowed imports

```
domain         ← (nothing project-internal)
application    ← domain
infrastructure ← domain, application
bot, api, wkr  ← application, domain, utils
composition    ← everything (it's the wiring)
utils          ← (nothing project-internal)
```

## Where to put a new file (decision tree)

- Telegram-facing UI (command, button, message reply) → `app/bot/...`
- Business rule, orchestration of multiple steps → `app/application/use_cases/...`
- Pure data class with invariants → `app/domain/entities/...`
- Talking to PG / Redis / disk / Telegram API / yt-dlp → `app/infrastructure/...`
- HTTP route → `app/api/...`
- Long-running OS process → `app/workers/...`
- Pure helper (no side effects) → `app/utils/...`
- Op tooling / install / backup / cleanup → `deploy/scripts/...`

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ Bot handler that calls `SQLAlchemy` / `redis` / `httpx` directly.
- ❌ `app/domain/*` imports `sqlalchemy`, `httpx`, `redis`, etc.
- ❌ Use case that imports from `app/bot/...` or `app/api/...`.
- ❌ "Util" function that opens a network connection or DB session.
- ❌ Global mutable state outside `composition.py`.
- ❌ Reading `os.environ` outside `app/config.py`.
